Place `left = 0` and `right = arr.length - 1`. At each step, evaluate the pair `(arr[left], arr[right])`:

- If the pair satisfies the condition, record the answer and move both inward.
- If the pair falls short (e.g., sum < target), move `left` right to increase the value.
- If the pair overshoots (e.g., sum > target), move `right` left to decrease the value.

## Why Sorted Input Is Required

When you move `left` right, you're claiming: 'no solution exists with `arr[left]` as the left element.' This is only safe to claim if you know that every element to the right of `left` is greater or equal - i.e., the array is sorted. On an unsorted array, skipping `arr[left]` might eliminate a valid pair.

## Example: Two Sum on Sorted Array

```javascript
function twoSumSorted(arr, target) {
  let left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
  while (left < right) {
    const sum = arr[left] + arr[right];
    if (sum === target) return [left, right];
    else if (sum < target) left++;   // need larger sum
    else right--;                    // need smaller sum
  }
  return [];
}
```

**Fast/slow pointers** both start at the head (or position 0). Slow advances one step per iteration; fast advances two steps. Two key applications:

## Cycle Detection (Floyd's Algorithm)

If a cycle exists, fast will eventually 'lap' slow and they will meet inside the cycle. If no cycle exists, fast will reach `null` first.

```javascript
function hasCycle(head) {
  let slow = head, fast = head;
  while (fast && fast.next) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow === fast) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

## Finding the Middle

When `fast` reaches the end of the list (or goes past it), `slow` is at the middle. For even-length lists, `slow` ends at the second-of-two middle nodes by default; check whether your problem wants the first or second middle.

## Kth Node from End

Advance `fast` exactly `k` steps ahead of `slow`. Then move both at the same speed until `fast` reaches the end. `slow` is now at the kth node from the end.

